## std::option_result — Option / Result helper-method FFI surface.
##
## #06.8 T#17: every method here was an `Option[...]_<m>` /
## `Result[...]_<m>` entry in the `name.starts_with("Option")` /
## `name.starts_with("Result")` arms of
## `compiler/ruxen_core/src/codegen/runtime_table/mod.rs` until T#21's
## namespace-anchor mechanism made it possible to attach FFI alias
## decls directly to the builtin `Option` / `Result` enums (both are
## registered as `DefKind::Enum` by `register_builtins`).
##
## ## Generic-stripping at the call site
##
## MIR mangles built-in method calls with the surface generic args:
## `Option[Int]_unwrap_or`, `Result[Foo,IoError]_is_ok`, etc. The
## bootstrap `class Option do lib ... end end` shell registers under the
## **generic-free** parent name, so the alias-map key is
## `Option_unwrap_or` / `Result_is_ok`. The lookup site in
## `mir/lower/expr/method_call.rs` falls back to a generic-stripped
## form when the exact-shape key misses — that fallback is what makes
## the entries below load-bearing.
##
## ## ABI notes
##
## Every runtime entry takes `void *` (an Option / Result box pointer)
## as its receiver. The lib decls declare `self` and let
## `register_class_lib_method` prepend `Ty::Class { name: "Option"|
## "Result" }` — a pointer-sized type at the wire level, identical to
## `Ty::Option(_)` / `Ty::Result(_,_)` at the cranelift ABI layer.
## Return types are declared `Int` for the void*-boxed and
## int64_t-bit-cast results; typeck still serves the user-facing
## generic-aware types from its hardcoded table in
## `typeck/method_resolvers`.
## ## Migrated surface returns (zero-Rust-stdlib bridge)
##
## The decls below carry the TRUE generic-aware surface returns that
## typeck delegates to via the builtin-receiver bridge
## (`is_delegated_head` now includes `Ty::Option`/`Ty::Result`;
## `method_home_key` maps them to `Option`/`Result`; element
## substitution into `T`/`E` is done by `substitute_generics_in_return`
## against these enums' declared params). Each return is ABI-identical
## to its C symbol's wire width — `T`/`E` by value, boxed `Option`, and
## `Bool` all cross the C ABI as I64, matching the int64_t-bit-cast
## results the `ruxen_option_*` / `ruxen_result_*` symbols return.
##
## Fully bridge-resolved (zero collections.rs arms):
##   * `map` / `map_err` — MIGRATED (Task H) to method-level generic
##     harvesting through the bridge. The decls below carry the transformed
##     type as a method-level generic (`map[U] -> Option[U]`/`Result[U, E]`,
##     `map_err[F] -> Result[T, F]`); `bridge_builtin_method` →
##     `harvest_and_subst_generics` binds it from the closure argument's
##     inferred return type (the same direct path `Array.map`/`zip[U]` use).
##     The collections.rs arms that minted a fresh type var (and the
##     `infer_combinator_block` that unified it) are RETIRED.
##   * `unwrap_or_else` — MIGRATED to `.rx` bodies below: return the success
##     payload on the present arm, run the fallback closure otherwise
##     (`match self { Some(x) -> x; nil -> f.() }`; Result passes the err to
##     `f.(e)`). The return is the success element `T`, a static declared
##     return that substitutes through the bridge — no resolver arm needed.
##
## RESIDUAL (still typed by collections.rs arms ahead of the bridge):
##   * `try_op` — the `?` operator desugaring (compiler intrinsic).
##   * `ok_or` — the err type comes from the ARG, not a static return
##     (like the pre-Task-H `map`); kept laundered for the FFI alias.
class Option[T]
  lib "../core/runtime/alloc.c"
    def unwrap_or as "ruxen_option_unwrap_or"(default: T) -> T
    def unwrap as "ruxen_option_unwrap" -> T
    def expect as "ruxen_option_expect"(msg: &String) -> T
    def present? as "ruxen_option_is_some" -> Bool
    def nil? as "ruxen_option_is_none" -> Bool
  end

  # Ruby `Option#ok_or` — convert to a `Result`, using `err` as the `Err`
  # payload when empty. MIGRATED (Task H) to a `.rx` body: the method-level
  # generic `E` is harvested from the `err` argument by the bridge
  # (`harvest_and_subst_generics`, the direct-arg path `zip[U]` also uses), so
  # the surface return `Result[T, E]` resolves from here — no laundered FFI
  # alias / resolver arm.
  def ok_or[E](err: E) -> Result[T, E]
    match self
      Some(x) -> Result.Ok(x)
      nil -> Result.Err(err)
    end
  end

  # Ruby `Option#map` — apply `f` to the contained value, repackage as
  # `Some`; propagate `nil` unchanged. Lowered ONCE as the opaque
  # `Option_map` body (T/U abstract). Exercises a closure call inside a
  # match arm of an opaque generic body.
  def map[U](f: any Fn[Fn(T) -> U]) -> Option[U]
    match self
      Some(x) -> Option.Some(f.(x))
      nil -> nil
    end
  end

  # Ruby `Option#unwrap_or_else` — return the contained value if present,
  # otherwise compute the fallback by calling `f` (no argument). Lowered
  # ONCE as the opaque `Option_unwrap_or_else` body (closure call in a
  # match arm of the opaque body, Bug 1 path).
  def unwrap_or_else(f: any Fn[Fn() -> T]) -> T
    match self
      Some(x) -> x
      nil -> f.()
    end
  end
end

class Result[T, E]
  lib "../core/runtime/alloc.c"
    def unwrap_or as "ruxen_result_unwrap_or"(default: T) -> T
    def unwrap as "ruxen_result_unwrap" -> T
    def expect as "ruxen_result_expect"(msg: &String) -> T
    def ok? as "ruxen_result_is_ok" -> Bool
    def err? as "ruxen_result_is_err" -> Bool
    def ok as "ruxen_result_ok" -> Option[T]
    def err as "ruxen_result_err" -> Option[E]
  end

  # Ruby `Result#map` — apply `f` to the `Ok` value, repackage as `Ok`;
  # propagate `Err` unchanged. Lowered ONCE as the opaque `Result_map`
  # body (T/U/E abstract). Closure call inside a match arm of an opaque
  # generic body.
  def map[U](f: any Fn[Fn(T) -> U]) -> Result[U, E]
    match self
      Ok(x) -> Result.Ok(f.(x))
      Err(e) -> Result.Err(e)
    end
  end

  # Ruby `Result#map_err` — apply `f` to the `Err` value; propagate `Ok`
  # unchanged.
  def map_err[F](f: any Fn[Fn(E) -> F]) -> Result[T, F]
    match self
      Ok(x) -> Result.Ok(x)
      Err(e) -> Result.Err(f.(e))
    end
  end

  # Ruby `Result#unwrap_or_else` — return the `Ok` value if present,
  # otherwise compute the fallback by calling `f` with the `Err` payload.
  # Lowered ONCE as the opaque `Result_unwrap_or_else` body (closure call
  # in a match arm of the opaque body, Bug 1 path).
  def unwrap_or_else(f: any Fn[Fn(E) -> T]) -> T
    match self
      Ok(x) -> x
      Err(e) -> f.(e)
    end
  end
end
